{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of silence that lives in libraries after hours, or in grocery stores just before they open. It's not empty. It's potential.\n\nI was thinking about how we treat waiting as wasted time. We stare at loading screens, at kettles that refuse to boil, at the three dots when someone is typing. But waiting is where the brain finally gets to breathe. When you're not inputting, you're processing.\n\nMy favorite thoughts have never arrived while I was grinding, focused, furiously typing. They arrived while I was doing dishes. While I was on a long bus ride with no service. While I was walking and forgot my headphones. The mind, unobserved, starts to wander and make those strange, lateral connections that feel like magic but are really just what happens when you let the system idle.\n\nWe built a world that hates idle. Every second must be optimized, monetized, filled with content. But creativity is not efficient. It needs a little boredom, a little uselessness. It needs you to stare out a window for ten minutes without feeling guilty.\n\nSo maybe today, don't fill every gap. Let one kettle boil unwatched. Let one walk be just a walk. See what shows up in the silence. That's where the good stuff has been hiding the whole time. \n\nAnd if nothing shows up, that's fine too. Not every silence has to produce something. Sometimes it's enough that it just existed, and you were there for it.",
